            <div class="mf-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="mf-node mf-leak"><div class="mf-icon">🌐</div><div class="mf-name">Offshore accounts<br>Panama · Cyprus · Liberia</div></div>
            <div class="mf-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="mf-node mf-leak"><div class="mf-icon">⚔️</div><div class="mf-name">FAR military elite</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="mf-footnote">An alleged $14.5–20B sits in undisclosed overseas accounts. GAESA's finances are not in Cuba's national budget. Cubans experience fuel shortages and blackouts.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ═══ STRUCTURE ═══ -->
    <div id="panel-structure" class="tab-panel">
      <div class="sec">
        <div class="sec-ey">Economic Control · What GAESA Owns</div>
        <h2 class="sec-headline">Cuba's Most Profitable Sectors — All Military-Controlled</h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">GAESA does not publish a corporate map. What is known comes from reporting, defector accounts, and the 2025 leaks revealing the V Department of the FAR — a secret unit that oversees GAESA operations. A 2015 Bloomberg profile described its then-chief as controlling "the largest business empire in Cuba."</p>
        <div class="sector-grid">
          <div class="sector-card">
            <div class="sc-icon">🏨</div>
            <div class="sc-name">Tourism</div>
            <div class="sc-desc">Controls most 5-star hotels. Primary hard-currency source. Foreign management companies operate the hotels; GAESA takes the revenue.</div>
            <div class="sc-subs">Gaviota S.A. · Cubanacán · Gran Caribe</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sector-card">
            <div class="sc-icon">🏦</div>
            <div class="sc-name">Banking</div>
            <div class="sc-desc">Controls Cuba's largest bank and key financial infrastructure including remittance processing.</div>
            <div class="sc-subs">RAFIN S.A. · Banco Financiero Internacional (BFI)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sector-card">
            <div class="sc-icon">⚓</div>
            <div class="sc-name">Port Logistics</div>
            <div class="sc-desc">Operates the Mariel Special Development Zone — Cuba's most modern port, partially built with Chinese investment.</div>
            <div class="sc-subs">Zona Especial de Desarrollo Mariel</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sector-card">
            <div class="sc-icon">🛒</div>
            <div class="sc-name">Retail</div>
            <div class="sc-desc">Operates much of Cuba's retail and supermarket network, including hard-currency stores inaccessible to most Cubans.</div>
            <div class="sc-subs">TRD Caribe · Cimex</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sector-card">
            <div class="sc-icon">⛽</div>
            <div class="sc-name">Fuel Distribution</div>
            <div class="sc-desc">Controls gas stations and fuel distribution while the Cuban population experiences acute shortages.</div>
            <div class="sc-subs">CUPET (partial)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sector-card">
            <div class="sc-icon">💸</div>
            <div class="sc-name">Remittances</div>
            <div class="sc-desc">Controls remittance processing infrastructure — taking a cut of billions sent from Cuban-Americans to family on the island.</div>
            <div class="sc-subs">Fincimex (sanctioned 2020) · AIS</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ═══ LEADERSHIP ═══ -->
